From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] ui: FullPanel, full screen over the strip beside the notch (experimental)

What: with OMACVM_FULLPANEL=1 (OmacVM.app sets it for one start, for a VM
whose notch setting is FullPanel), a full-screen window on a display with a
camera housing covers the whole display, the strip beside the notch
included, and stays a real macOS full-screen window in a Space of its own.
AppKit's private full-screen frame (-_frameForFullScreenMode, hooked on
QemuWindow and OmacVMHeadWindow) becomes the whole display while the
window has the display to itself, and stays so for that full screen;
frame changes AppKit tries during relayouts are refused, Split View still
goes through. The full-screen Space's menu bar is made transparent
(SkyLight, that Space only) and comes down from the display's top row as
usual; AppKit's full-screen toolbar window is hidden with it. The guest's
size, the full-screen area and the display arrangement sent to the guest
then start at the display's top edge, and the guest is the whole panel:
no rows cut for Omarchy's scale presets (omacvm-cocoa-clean-size.patch
does that below a notch; over the strip the cut rows were a black band at
the bottom, 2940x1900 on a 2940x1912 MacBook Air). Rules in
ui/omacvm-fullpanel.h (omacvm-cocoa-fullpanel-logic.patch). qemu.log:
"omacvm: full panel: strip covered: ..." per full screen that has it
(omacvm check reads it), "strip lost" when AppKit moved the window below
the notch after all.

Why: OmacVM.app's own full screen can draw beside the camera itself, so on
that route the guest's bar can sit in the strip without Omanotch's stream
(Omanotch stays the default and the only way on UTM, VMware Fusion and
Parallels, which keep their windows below the notch). Issue #339.

Fallbacks: without OMACVM_FULLPANEL=1 nothing of it runs. External
displays (no camera housing), macOS's "Always show the menu bar in full
screen", and any private class, selector, function or type encoding that
is missing or changed give normal full screen, logged once ("using normal
full screen"). A full screen that loses the strip is not forced back.

Credit: Brian Merchant's FullPanel branch for OmacVM (#339): the frame
hook, the reveal handling and the fallbacks follow it. That work follows
UTM's camera-housing full screen (Turing Software, LLC; UTM PR #7885 and
#7910, Apache-2.0) as an independent Objective-C implementation; no UTM
code is copied.

Upstream: not applicable (private AppKit/SkyLight interfaces, OmacVM's notch).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/display/test-fullpanel.sh (the rules, every
notched MacBook's size, external displays, moves between them); the
runtime build compiles it; the strip itself only on a notched Mac
(docs/features.md, FullPanel).

